descendIdeal -- finds the maximal F-pure Cartier submodule of an ideal viewed as a Cartier module
*************************************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        descendIdeal(e, tList, fList, J)
  * Inputs:
      * e, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, the order of the Frobenius root
        to take
      * fList, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, consisting of ring elements
        f_1,\ldots,f_n, for a pair
      * tList, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, consisting of formal
        exponents t_1,\ldots,t_n for the elements of fList
      * J, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, the Cartier module to study
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * FrobeniusRootStrategy => a *note symbol: (Macaulay2Doc)Symbol,, default
        value Substitution, selects the strategy for internal frobeniusRoot
        calls
  * Outputs:
      * a *note sequence: (Macaulay2Doc)Sequence,, whose first entry is the
        maximal $F$-pure Cartier submodule of J under the dual-e-iterated
        Frobenius induced by f_1^{t_1}\ldots f_n^{t_n}, and the second entry is
        the number of times frobeniusRoot was applied

Description
===========

This command computes the maximal $F$-pure Cartier submodule of an ideal $J$
under the dual-$e$-iterated Frobenius induced by $f_1^{t_1}\ldots f_n^{t_n}$.

The function returns a sequence, where the first entry is the descended ideal,
and the second entry is the number of times frobeniusRoot was applied (i.e.,
the HSL number).

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = ZZ/7[x,y,z];                                                        |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : f = y^2 - x^3;                                                          |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : descendIdeal(1, {5}, {f}, ideal 1_R) --this computes the non-F-pure     |
|                                                                             |
|o3 = (ideal 1, 0)                                                            |
|                                                                             |
|o3 : Sequence                                                                |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|ideal of (R, f^{5/6})                                                        |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : descendIdeal(2, {41}, {f}, ideal 1_R) --this computes the non-F-pure    |
|                                                                             |
|o4 = (ideal (y, x), 1)                                                       |
|                                                                             |
|o4 : Sequence                                                                |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|ideal of (R, f^{41/48})                                                      |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

The same two examples could also be accomplished via calls of FPureModule, as
illustrated below; however, the descendIdeal construction gives the user more
direct control.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : first FPureModule(5/6, f, CanonicalIdeal => ideal 1_R, GeneratorList => |
|                                                                             |
|o5 = ideal 1                                                                 |
|                                                                             |
|o5 : Ideal of R                                                              |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|{1_R})                                                                       |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : first FPureModule(41/48, f, CanonicalIdeal => ideal 1_R, GeneratorList  |
|                                                                             |
|o6 = ideal (y, x)                                                            |
|                                                                             |
|o6 : Ideal of R                                                              |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|=> {1_R})                                                                    |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

The option FrobeniusRootStrategy is passed to internal *note frobeniusRoot:
frobeniusRoot, calls.

See also
========

  * *note FPureModule: FPureModule, -- compute the submodule of the canonical
    module stable under the image of the trace of Frobenius
  * *note ascendIdeal: ascendIdeal, -- find the smallest ideal containing a
    given ideal which is compatible with a given Cartier linear map

Ways to use descendIdeal:
=========================

  * "descendIdeal(ZZ,List,List,Ideal)"

