File: TestIdeals.info, Node: isFPure, Next: isFRational, Prev: isFInjective, Up: Top

isFPure -- whether a ring is F-pure
***********************************

  * Usage: 
        isFPure(R)
        isFPure(I)
  * Inputs:
      * R, a *note ring: (Macaulay2Doc)Ring,, whose $F$-purity is to be checked
      * I, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, the defining ideal of the ring
        whose $F$-purity is to be checked
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * AtOrigin => a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, default
        value false, specifies whether the $F$-purity is to be checked only at
        the origin
      * FrobeniusRootStrategy => a *note symbol: (Macaulay2Doc)Symbol,, default
        value Substitution, selects the strategy for internal frobeniusRoot
        calls
  * Outputs:
      * a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, 

Description
===========

Given a ring $R$, this function checks whether the ring is $F$-pure, using
Fedder's criterion (by applying *note frobeniusRoot: frobeniusRoot, to $I^{[p]}
: I$, where $I$ is the defining ideal of $R$).

+---------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = ZZ/5[x,y,z]/(x^2 + y*z);      |
+---------------------------------------+
|i2 : isFPure(R)                        |
|                                       |
|o2 = true                              |
+---------------------------------------+
|i3 : R = ZZ/7[x,y,z]/(x^3 + y^3 + z^3);|
+---------------------------------------+
|i4 : isFPure(R)                        |
|                                       |
|o4 = true                              |
+---------------------------------------+
|i5 : R = ZZ/5[x,y,z]/(x^3 + y^3 + z^3);|
+---------------------------------------+
|i6 : isFPure(R)                        |
|                                       |
|o6 = false                             |
+---------------------------------------+

Alternatively, one may pass the defining ideal of a ring.

+-----------------------------------------------+
|i7 : S = ZZ/2[x,y,z];                          |
+-----------------------------------------------+
|i8 : isFPure ideal(y^2 - x^3)                  |
|                                               |
|o8 = false                                     |
+-----------------------------------------------+
|i9 : isFPure ideal(z^2 - x*y*z + x*y^2 + x^2*y)|
|                                               |
|o9 = true                                      |
+-----------------------------------------------+

The option AtOrigin controls whether $F$-purity is checked at the origin or
everywhere. If its value is set to true (the default is false), it will only
check $F$-purity at the origin.

+----------------------------------------------------+
|i10 : R = ZZ/5[x,y,z]/((x - 1)^3 + (y - 2)^3 + z^3);|
+----------------------------------------------------+
|i11 : isFPure(R)                                    |
|                                                    |
|o11 = false                                         |
+----------------------------------------------------+
|i12 : isFPure(R, AtOrigin => true)                  |
|                                                    |
|o12 = true                                          |
+----------------------------------------------------+
|i13 : S = ZZ/13[x,y,z]/(x^3 + y^3 + z^3);           |
+----------------------------------------------------+
|i14 : isFPure(S)                                    |
|                                                    |
|o14 = true                                          |
+----------------------------------------------------+
|i15 : isFPure(S, AtOrigin => true)                  |
|                                                    |
|o15 = true                                          |
+----------------------------------------------------+

Note that there is a difference in the strategy for the local or non-local
computations. In fact, checking $F$-purity globally can sometimes be faster
than checking it only at the origin. If AtOrigin is set to false, then isFPure
computes the *note frobeniusRoot: frobeniusRoot, of $I^{[p]} : I$, whereas if
AtOrigin is set to true, it checks whether $I^{[p]} : I$ is contained in
$m^{[p]}$, where $m$ is the maximal ideal generated by the variables.

The option FrobeniusRootStrategy is passed to internal *note frobeniusRoot:
frobeniusRoot, calls, and specifies the strategy to be used.

See also
========

  * *note isFRegular: isFRegular, -- whether a ring or pair is strongly
    F-regular
  * *note isFInjective: isFInjective, -- whether a ring is F-injective

Ways to use isFPure:
====================

  * "isFPure(Ideal)"
  * "isFPure(Ring)"

For the programmer
==================

The object *note isFPure: isFPure, is a *note method function with options:
(Macaulay2Doc)MethodFunctionWithOptions,.

