File: TestIdeals.info, Node: isFRational, Next: isFRegular, Prev: isFPure, Up: Top

isFRational -- whether a ring is F-rational
*******************************************

  * Usage: 
        isFRational(R)
  * Inputs:
      * R, a *note ring: (Macaulay2Doc)Ring,, 
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * AtOrigin => a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, default
        value false, specifies that $F$-rationality be checked only at the
        origin, and that the Cohen-Macaulayness test be done with the isCM
        command, from the Depth package
      * AssumeCM => a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, default
        value false, assumes the ring is Cohen-Macaulay
      * AssumeDomain => a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, default
        value false, assumes the ring is an integral domain
      * FrobeniusRootStrategy => a *note symbol: (Macaulay2Doc)Symbol,, default
        value Substitution, selects the strategy for internal frobeniusRoot
        calls
  * Outputs:
      * a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, 

Description
===========

The function isFRational determines whether a ring is $F$-rational. If the
option AtOrigin (default value false) is set to true, it will only check if the
ring is $F$-rational at the origin (this can be slower). If the option AssumeCM
(default value false) is set to true, it will not verify that the ring is
Cohen-Macaulay.

+---------------------------------------------+
|i1 : T = ZZ/5[x,y];                          |
+---------------------------------------------+
|i2 : S = ZZ/5[a,b,c,d];                      |
+---------------------------------------------+
|i3 : g = map(T, S, {x^3, x^2*y, x*y^2, y^3});|
|                                             |
|o3 : RingMap T <-- S                         |
+---------------------------------------------+
|i4 : R = S/(ker g);                          |
+---------------------------------------------+
|i5 : isFRational(R)                          |
|                                             |
|o5 = true                                    |
+---------------------------------------------+
+---------------------------------------+
|i6 : R = ZZ/7[x,y,z]/(x^3 + y^3 + z^3);|
+---------------------------------------+
|i7 : isFRational(R)                    |
|                                       |
|o7 = false                             |
+---------------------------------------+

Below is a more interesting example, of a ring that is $F$-rational but not
$F$-regular. This example first appeared in A. K. Singh's work on deformation
of $F$-regularity.

+------------------------------------------------------+
|i8 : S = ZZ/3[a,b,c,d,t];                             |
+------------------------------------------------------+
|i9 : M = matrix{{a^2 + t^4, b, d}, {c, a^2, b^3 - d}};|
|                                                      |
|             2      3                                 |
|o9 : Matrix S  <-- S                                  |
+------------------------------------------------------+
|i10 : I = minors(2, M);                               |
|                                                      |
|o10 : Ideal of S                                      |
+------------------------------------------------------+
|i11 : R = S/I;                                        |
+------------------------------------------------------+
|i12 : isFRational(R)                                  |
|                                                      |
|o12 = true                                            |
+------------------------------------------------------+

The option AssumeDomain is used when computing a test element.  The option
FrobeniusRootStrategy is passed to internal *note frobeniusRoot: frobeniusRoot,
calls.

Caveat
======

This function assumes that the spectrum of the ring is connected. Like
isCohenMacaulay, if given a non-equidimensional $F$-rational ring (e.g., a ring
whose spectrum has two connected components of different dimensions),
isFRational will return false.

Ways to use isFRational:
========================

  * "isFRational(Ring)"

For the programmer
==================

The object *note isFRational: isFRational, is a *note method function with
options: (Macaulay2Doc)MethodFunctionWithOptions,.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The source of this document is in TestIdeals/parameterTestIdealDoc.m2:316:0.

File: TestIdeals.info, Node: isFRegular, Next: Katzman, Prev: isFRational, Up: Top

isFRegular -- whether a ring or pair is strongly F-regular
**********************************************************

  * Usage: 
        isFRegular(R)
        isFRegular(t, f)
        isFRegular(tList, fList)
  * Inputs:
      * R, a *note ring: (Macaulay2Doc)Ring,, 
      * t, a *note rational number: (Macaulay2Doc)QQ,, a formal exponent for f
      * f, a *note ring element: (Macaulay2Doc)RingElement,, the element for
        the pair, to test $F$-regularity
      * tList, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, a list of formal exponents,
        for the elements of fList
      * fList, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, a list of elements for a
        pair, to test $F$-regularity
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * AssumeDomain => a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, default
        value false, assumes the ring is an integral domain
      * FrobeniusRootStrategy => a *note symbol: (Macaulay2Doc)Symbol,, default
        value Substitution, selects the strategy for internal frobeniusRoot
        calls
      * MaxCartierIndex => an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, default value
        10, sets the maximum Gorenstein index to search for when working with a
        $\mathbb{Q}$-Gorenstein ambient ring
      * QGorensteinIndex => an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, default value
        0, specifies the $\mathbb{Q}$-Gorenstein index of the ring
      * AtOrigin => a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, default
        value false, specifies whether to check $F$-regularity just at the
        origin
      * DepthOfSearch => an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, default value 2,
        specifies how hard to try to prove that a non-$\mathbb{Q}$-Gorenstein
        ring is $F$-regular
  * Outputs:
      * a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, 

Description
===========

Given a normal $\mathbb{Q}$-Gorenstein ring $R$, the function isFRegular checks
whether the ring is strongly $F$-regular. It can also prove that a
non-$\mathbb{Q}$-Gorenstein ring is $F$-regular (but cannot show it is not);
see below for how to access this functionality.

+---------------------------------+
|i1 : R = ZZ/5[x,y,z]/(x^2 + y*z);|
+---------------------------------+
|i2 : isFRegular(R)               |
|                                 |
|o2 = true                        |
+---------------------------------+
+---------------------------------------+
|i3 : R = ZZ/7[x,y,z]/(x^3 + y^3 + z^3);|
+---------------------------------------+
|i4 : isFRegular(R)                     |
|                                       |
|o4 = false                             |
+---------------------------------------+

The function isFRegular can also test strong $F$-regularity of pairs.

+-----------------------------------+
|i5 : R = ZZ/5[x,y];                |
+-----------------------------------+
|i6 : f = y^2 - x^3;                |
+-----------------------------------+
|i7 : isFRegular(1/2, f)            |
|                                   |
|o7 = true                          |
+-----------------------------------+
|i8 : isFRegular(5/6, f)            |
|                                   |
|o8 = false                         |
+-----------------------------------+
|i9 : isFRegular(4/5, f)            |
|                                   |
|o9 = false                         |
+-----------------------------------+
|i10 : isFRegular(4/5 - 1/100000, f)|
|                                   |
|o10 = true                         |
+-----------------------------------+

When checking whether a ring or pair is strongly $F$-regular, the option
AtOrigin determines if this is to be checked at the origin or everywhere. The
default value for AtOrigin is false, which corresponds to checking
$F$-regularity everywhere; setting AtOrigin => true, $F$-regularity is checked
only at the origin.

+----------------------------------------------------+
|i11 : R = ZZ/7[x,y,z]/((x - 1)^3 + (y + 1)^3 + z^3);|
+----------------------------------------------------+
|i12 : isFRegular(R)                                 |
|                                                    |
|o12 = false                                         |
+----------------------------------------------------+
|i13 : isFRegular(R, AtOrigin => true)               |
|                                                    |
|o13 = true                                          |
+----------------------------------------------------+
|i14 : S = ZZ/13[x,y,z]/(x^3 + y^3 + z^3);           |
+----------------------------------------------------+
|i15 : isFRegular(S)                                 |
|                                                    |
|o15 = false                                         |
+----------------------------------------------------+
|i16 : isFRegular(S, AtOrigin => true)               |
|                                                    |
|o16 = false                                         |
+----------------------------------------------------+

Here is an example of AtOrigin behavior with a pair.

+------------------------------------------+
|i17 : R = ZZ/13[x,y];                     |
+------------------------------------------+
|i18 : f = (y - 2)^2 - (x - 3)^3;          |
+------------------------------------------+
|i19 : isFRegular(5/6, f)                  |
|                                          |
|o19 = false                               |
+------------------------------------------+
|i20 : isFRegular(5/6, f, AtOrigin => true)|
|                                          |
|o20 = true                                |
+------------------------------------------+
|i21 : g = y^2 - x^3;                      |
+------------------------------------------+
|i22 : isFRegular(5/6, g)                  |
|                                          |
|o22 = false                               |
+------------------------------------------+
|i23 : isFRegular(5/6, g, AtOrigin => true)|
|                                          |
|o23 = false                               |
+------------------------------------------+

The option AssumeDomain (default value false) is used when finding a test
element. The option FrobeniusRootStrategy (default value Substitution) is
passed to internal *note frobeniusRoot: frobeniusRoot, calls.

When working in a $\mathbb{Q}$-Gorenstein ring $R$, isFRegular looks for a
positive integer $N$ such that $N K_R$ is Cartier. The option MaxCartierIndex
(default value $10$) controls the maximum value of $N$ to consider in this
search. If the smallest such $N$ turns out to be greater than the value passed
to MaxCartierIndex, then testIdeal returns an error.

The $\mathbb{Q}$-Gorenstein index can be specified by the user through the
option QGorensteinIndex; when this option is used, the search for $N$ is
bypassed, and the option MaxCartierIndex ignored.

The function isFRegular can show that rings that are not
$\mathbb{Q}$-Gorenstein are $F$-regular (it cannot, however, show that such a
ring is not $F$-regular). To do this, set the option QGorensteinIndex =>
infinity. One may also use the option DepthOfSearch to increase the depth of
search.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i24 : S = ZZ/7[x,y,z,u,v,w];                                                 |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i25 : I = minors(2, matrix {{x, y, z}, {u, v, w}});                          |
|                                                                             |
|o25 : Ideal of S                                                             |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i26 : debugLevel = 1;                                                        |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i27 : time isFRegular(S/I, QGorensteinIndex => infinity,                     |
|isFRegular: This ring does not appear to be F-regular.                       |
| -- used 0.134789s (cpu); 0.0897126s (thread); 0s (gc)                       |
|                                                                             |
|o27 = false                                                                  |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| DepthOfSearch => 1)                                                         |
|Increasing DepthOfSearch will let the function search more deeply.           |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i28 : time isFRegular(S/I, QGorensteinIndex => infinity, DepthOfSearch => 2) |
| -- used 0.184753s (cpu); 0.184755s (thread); 0s (gc)                        |
|                                                                             |
|o28 = true                                                                   |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i29 : debugLevel = 0;                                                        |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

