testIdeal -- compute a test ideal in a Q-Gorenstein ring
********************************************************

  * Usage: 
        testIdeal(R)
        testIdeal(t, f)
        testIdeal(tList, fList)
  * Inputs:
      * R, a *note ring: (Macaulay2Doc)Ring,, a $\mathbb{Q}$-Gorenstein ring
      * f, a *note ring element: (Macaulay2Doc)RingElement,, the element in a
        pair
      * t, a *note number: (Macaulay2Doc)Number,, the formal exponent to which
        f is raised
      * fList, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, consisting of ring elements
        f_1,\ldots,f_n, for a pair
      * tList, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, consisting of formal
        exponents t_1,\ldots,t_n for the elements of fList
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * AssumeDomain => a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, default
        value false, assumes the ring is an integral domain
      * FrobeniusRootStrategy => a *note symbol: (Macaulay2Doc)Symbol,, default
        value Substitution, selects the strategy for internal frobeniusRoot
        calls
      * MaxCartierIndex => an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, default value
        10, sets the maximum $\mathbb{Q}$-Gorenstein index to search for
      * QGorensteinIndex => an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, default value
        0, specifies the $\mathbb{Q}$-Gorenstein index of the ring
  * Outputs:
      * an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, the test ideal \tau(R),
        \tau(R,f^{t}), or \tau(R,f_1^{t_1}\ldots f_n^{t_n}), depending on the
        arguments passed

Description
===========

Given a normal $\mathbb{Q}$-Gorenstein ring $R$, testIdeal(R) simply computes
the test ideal \tau($R$).

+---------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = ZZ/5[x,y,z]/(x^3 + y^3 + z^3);|
+---------------------------------------+
|i2 : testIdeal(R)                      |
|                                       |
|o2 = ideal (z, y, x)                   |
|                                       |
|o2 : Ideal of R                        |
+---------------------------------------+
+---------------------------------------------+
|i3 : S = ZZ/5[x,y,z,w];                      |
+---------------------------------------------+
|i4 : T = ZZ/5[a,b];                          |
+---------------------------------------------+
|i5 : f = map(T, S, {a^3, a^2*b, a*b^2, b^3});|
|                                             |
|o5 : RingMap T <-- S                         |
+---------------------------------------------+
|i6 : R = S/(ker f);                          |
+---------------------------------------------+
|i7 : testIdeal(R)                            |
|                                             |
|o7 = ideal 1                                 |
|                                             |
|o7 : Ideal of R                              |
+---------------------------------------------+

Given a nonnegative rational number $t$ and an element $f$ of a normal
$\mathbb{Q}$-Gorenstein ring $R$, testIdeal(t,f) computes the test ideal
\tau($R$, $f^{ t}$).

+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i8 : R = ZZ/5[x,y,z];                                      |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i9 : f = y^2 - x^3;                                        |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i10 : apply({1/2, 4/5, 5/6, 1}, t -> testIdeal(t, f) )     |
|                                                           |
|                                                   3    2  |
|o10 = {ideal 1, ideal (y, x), ideal (y, x), ideal(x  - y )}|
|                                                           |
|o10 : List                                                 |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i11 : R = ZZ/7[x,y,z];                                     |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i12 : f = y^2 - x^3;                                       |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+
|i13 : apply({1/2, 4/5, 5/6, 1}, t -> testIdeal(t, f) )     |
|                                                           |
|                                              3    2       |
|o13 = {ideal 1, ideal 1, ideal (y, x), ideal(x  - y )}     |
|                                                           |
|o13 : List                                                 |
+-----------------------------------------------------------+

The ring $R$ need not be a polynomial ring, as the next example illustrates.

+-----------------------------------+
|i14 : R = ZZ/11[x,y,z]/(x^2 - y*z);|
+-----------------------------------+
|i15 : testIdeal(1/2, y)            |
|                                   |
|o15 = ideal (y, x)                 |
|                                   |
|o15 : Ideal of R                   |
+-----------------------------------+
|i16 : testIdeal(1/3, y)            |
|                                   |
|o16 = ideal 1                      |
|                                   |
|o16 : Ideal of R                   |
+-----------------------------------+

Given nonnegative rational numbers $t_1, t_2, \ldots$ and ring elements $f_1,
f_2, \ldots$, testIdeal(\{t_1,t_2,\ldots\},\{f_1,f_2,\ldots\}) computes the
test ideal \tau($R$, $f_1^{t_1} f_2^{t_2}\cdots$).

+-----------------------------------+
|i17 : R = ZZ/7[x,y];               |
+-----------------------------------+
|i18 : L = {x, y, x + y};           |
+-----------------------------------+
|i19 : f = x*y*(x + y);             |
+-----------------------------------+
|i20 : testIdeal({2/3, 2/3, 2/3}, L)|
|                                   |
|o20 = ideal (y, x)                 |
|                                   |
|o20 : Ideal of R                   |
+-----------------------------------+
|i21 : testIdeal(2/3, f)            |
|                                   |
|o21 = ideal (y, x)                 |
|                                   |
|o21 : Ideal of R                   |
+-----------------------------------+
|i22 : testIdeal({3/4, 2/3, 3/5}, L)|
|                                   |
|o22 = ideal (y, x)                 |
|                                   |
|o22 : Ideal of R                   |
+-----------------------------------+

It is often more efficient to pass a list, as opposed to finding a common
denominator and passing a single element, since testIdeal can do things in a
more intelligent way for such a list.

+-----------------------------------------------------+
|i23 : time testIdeal({3/4, 2/3, 3/5}, L)             |
| -- used 0.24125s (cpu); 0.199373s (thread); 0s (gc) |
|                                                     |
|o23 = ideal (y, x)                                   |
|                                                     |
|o23 : Ideal of R                                     |
+-----------------------------------------------------+
|i24 : time testIdeal(1/60, x^45*y^40*(x + y)^36)     |
| -- used 0.321067s (cpu); 0.283391s (thread); 0s (gc)|
|                                                     |
|o24 = ideal (y, x)                                   |
|                                                     |
|o24 : Ideal of R                                     |
+-----------------------------------------------------+

The option AssumeDomain (default value false) is used when finding a test
element. The option FrobeniusRootStrategy (default value Substitution) is
passed to internal *note frobeniusRoot: frobeniusRoot, calls.

When working in a $\mathbb{Q}$-Gorenstein ring $R$, testIdeal looks for a
positive integer $N$ such that $N K_R$ is Cartier. The option MaxCartierIndex
(default value $10$) controls the maximum value of $N$ to consider in this
search. If the smallest such $N$ turns out to be greater than the value passed
to MaxCartierIndex, then testIdeal returns an error.

The $\mathbb{Q}$-Gorenstein index can be specified by the user through the
option QGorensteinIndex; when this option is used, the search for $N$ is
bypassed, and the option MaxCartierIndex ignored.

See also
========

  * *note testModule: testModule, -- find the parameter test module of a
    reduced ring
  * *note isFRegular: isFRegular, -- whether a ring or pair is strongly
    F-regular

Ways to use testIdeal:
======================

  * "testIdeal(List,List)"
  * "testIdeal(Number,RingElement)"
  * "testIdeal(Ring)"

For the programmer
==================

The object *note testIdeal: testIdeal, is a *note method function with options:
